\title{The Automorphism $2$-Group of an Arbitrary Homotopy $1$-Type}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{5 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete algebraic description of the homotopy theory of homotopy $1$-types, working in the groupoid model in which a $1$-type is a groupoid, a map is a functor, and a homotopy is a natural isomorphism. Our starting point is the classification of connected $1$-types by their fundamental group, together with the identification $[K(G,1),K(H,1)] \cong \Hom(G,H)/\text{conj}$. We upgrade this set-level statement to an algebraic one: for a connected $1$-type $X$ with $\pi_1 X = G$, the monoid $[X,X]$ of homotopy classes of self-maps is isomorphic to the monoid $\ConjEnd(G)$ of conjugacy classes of endomorphisms of $G$; a class is invertible precisely when it is represented by an equivalence; and therefore $\hAut(X)\cong\Out(G)$, while the self-homotopies of the identity form the centre $Z(G)$. This determines the automorphism $2$-group of every connected $1$-type. We then remove connectedness entirely. For an arbitrary family $(C_i)_{i\in\iota}$ of connected $1$-types we prove a matrix normal form: $\bigl[\bigsqcup_i C_i,\bigsqcup_i C_i\bigr]$ is isomorphic to the monoid of pairs $\langle\sigma,P\rangle$ with $\sigma:\iota\to\iota$ and $P_i\in[C_i,C_{\sigma(i)}]$. Passing to units yields the exact sequence
\[ 1 \to \prod_i \Out(\pi_1 C_i) \to \hAut\Bigl(\bigsqcup_i C_i\Bigr) \to \Sym'(\pi_0) \to 1, \]
where $\Sym'(\pi_0)$ consists of the permutations of the components preserving homotopy type; the self-homotopies of the identity form $\prod_i Z(\pi_1 C_i)$. We recover both extremes---the wreath product $\Out(G)\wr\Sym(\iota)$ for a constant family and the symmetric group $\Sym(\pi_0)$ for a discrete family---and give explicit computations: $\hAut(S^1)\cong\ZZ/2$ with degree monoid $(\ZZ,\cdot)$; $\hAut(T^n)\cong\GL_n(\ZZ)$; $\hAut(K(\ZZ/n,1))\cong(\ZZ/n)^\times$ of order $\varphi(n)$; rigidity of $K(S_3,1)$; and the four-element symmetry group of $K(\ZZ,1)\sqcup K(\ZZ/3,1)$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} Eilenberg--MacLane space, aspherical space, fundamental group, groupoid, outer automorphism group, homotopy self-equivalence, wreath product, Euler totient function.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

The fundamental group is the oldest algebraic invariant of a topological space, and it is famously incomplete: $S^2$ and a point have the same fundamental group and different homotopy types. It is therefore natural to isolate the class of spaces on which $\pi_1$ \emph{is} complete, and to ask how much of the homotopy theory of that class is visible in group theory alone.

The class is well known: the \emph{homotopy $1$-types}, i.e.\ spaces all of whose higher homotopy groups vanish. A connected one with fundamental group $G$ is an Eilenberg--MacLane space $K(G,1)$, also called aspherical; such spaces exist for every group and are unique up to homotopy equivalence. What is less often spelled out in full is the \emph{complete} dictionary: not only the objects, but the mapping spaces, the self-equivalence groups, and the higher automorphism structure---and not only in the connected case. The purpose of this paper is to write that dictionary down in complete generality, with no connectedness assumptions anywhere, and to demonstrate it on explicit examples.

\subsection{The groupoid model}

Throughout we adopt the algebraic model of homotopy $1$-types. Given a space $X$, its fundamental groupoid $\Pi_1X$ has the points of $X$ as objects and homotopy classes of paths as morphisms. This construction induces an equivalence between the homotopy category of $1$-types and the homotopy category of groupoids, under which:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
topology & groupoid model \\
\midrule
space & groupoid $C$ \\
path component set $\pi_0X$ & set of isomorphism classes of objects \\
$\pi_1(X,x)$ & vertex group $\Aut(x)$ \\
continuous map & functor \\
homotopy of maps & natural isomorphism of functors \\
homotopy equivalence & equivalence of categories \\
$K(G,1)$ & connected groupoid with vertex group $G$ \\
disjoint union & coproduct of groupoids \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

We therefore use ``$1$-type'' and ``groupoid'' interchangeably, ``map'' for ``functor'', and ``homotopic'' for ``naturally isomorphic''. The simplest model of $K(G,1)$ is the \emph{one-object groupoid} $BG$ with a single object $\star$ and $\Aut(\star)=G$.

\subsection{Notation}

For groupoids $A,B$ write $[A,B]$ for the set of natural-isomorphism classes of functors $A\to B$, and $\clsof{F}$ for the class of $F$. Composition makes $[A,A]$ a monoid, denoted $\hEnd(A)$; we write $\hAut(A):=\hEnd(A)^\times$ for its group of units. For a group $G$ we use $\Aut(G),\Inn(G),\Out(G)=\Aut(G)/\Inn(G)$ and $Z(G)$ with their usual meanings, and $\ConjEnd(G):=\End(G)/\!\sim$ for the endomorphisms modulo $\phi\sim c_a\circ\phi$ with $c_a(g)=aga^{-1}$. A groupoid $C$ is \emph{connected at $c$} if every object is isomorphic to $c$.

\section{The connected case}

\subsection{Classification of objects and maps}

\begin{theorem}[Complete invariance]\label{thm:inv}
Two connected $1$-types are homotopy equivalent if and only if their fundamental groups are isomorphic. More precisely, if $C$ is connected at $c$ and $D$ at $d$, then a group isomorphism $\Aut(c)\cong\Aut(d)$ gives rise to an equivalence $C\simeq D$, and conversely every equivalence $C\simeq D$ induces such an isomorphism.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Connectedness lets one choose, for each object $x$, an isomorphism $u_x:c\to x$ with $u_c=\mathrm{id}$. These choices exhibit an equivalence between $C$ and the one-object groupoid on $\Aut(c)$, and similarly for $D$. A group isomorphism $\theta:\Aut(c)\to\Aut(d)$ is exactly an isomorphism of the two one-object groupoids, so composing the three equivalences gives $C\simeq D$. Conversely an equivalence $E:C\simeq D$ induces $\Aut(c)\to\Aut(Ec)$ by functoriality (an isomorphism because $E$ is fully faithful), and conjugating by a chosen isomorphism $d\to Ec$ lands in $\Aut(d)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Classification of maps]\label{thm:maps}
For connected $1$-types $C$ (at $c$) and $D$ (at $d$) with $G=\Aut(c)$, $H=\Aut(d)$, there is a natural bijection
\[ [C,D]\;\cong\;\Hom(G,H)/\text{conjugation by }H. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A functor $F:C\to D$ induces $\Aut(c)\to\Aut(Fc)$; a choice of isomorphism $p:d\to Fc$ transports this to $\iota_p(F):G\to H$. Changing $p$ conjugates $\iota_p(F)$, so its conjugacy class is well defined, and a natural isomorphism $F\cong F'$ supplies a compatible comparison, so the class depends only on $\clsof F$. Conversely a homomorphism $\phi:G\to H$ determines a functor $BG\to BH$; composing with the equivalences $C\simeq BG$ and $BH\simeq D$ gives a functor $C\to D$. The two constructions are mutually inverse.
\end{proof}

The conjugation quotient is the exact shadow of basepoint-freeness: conjugating a homomorphism corresponds to dragging the image of the basepoint around a loop, which is a homotopy.

\subsection{The self-map monoid}

\begin{theorem}[Monoid classification]\label{thm:monoid}
For a connected $1$-type $C$ at $c$ with $G=\Aut(c)$, the bijection of Theorem~\ref{thm:maps} is an isomorphism of monoids
\[ \hEnd(C)\;\cong\;\ConjEnd(G). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The induced-homomorphism construction sends the identity functor to the class of $\mathrm{id}_G$ and a composite $F\circ F'$ to the composite of the classes; the auxiliary chosen path changes by conjugation under composition, which is invisible after passing to conjugacy classes. Bijectivity is Theorem~\ref{thm:maps}. That $\ConjEnd(G)$ is a monoid follows from $c_a\phi\circ c_b\psi=c_{a\phi(b)}(\phi\psi)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Invertibility criterion]\label{thm:whitehead}
A class $\clsof F\in\hEnd(C)$ is invertible if and only if $F$ is an equivalence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $F$ is an equivalence with quasi-inverse $F^{-1}$, the unit and counit isomorphisms give $\clsof F\clsof{F^{-1}}=\clsof{F^{-1}}\clsof F=1$. Conversely, invertibility supplies natural isomorphisms $G\circ F\cong\mathrm{id}$ and $F\circ G\cong\mathrm{id}$, which are the data of an equivalence after the standard triangle-identity correction.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:whitehead} holds for arbitrary $1$-types, not merely connected ones.

\subsection{The main theorem in the connected case}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetry theorem]\label{thm:out}
Let $C$ be a $1$-type connected at $c$, with $G=\Aut(c)$. Then
\[ \hAut(C)\;\cong\;\Out(G)=\Aut(G)/\Inn(G), \]
and in particular $\#\hAut(C)=\#\Out(G)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:monoid}, $\hAut(C)\cong\ConjEnd(G)^\times$. An endomorphism whose class is invertible must be an automorphism, since composing with a representative of the inverse class yields an inner automorphism, which is bijective. Two automorphisms are conjugate exactly when they differ by an inner automorphism, so $\ConjEnd(G)^\times\cong\Aut(G)/\Inn(G)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Self-homotopies of the identity]\label{thm:centre}
For a $1$-type $C$ connected at $c$, the group $\Aut(\mathrm{id}_C)$ of self-homotopies of the identity is isomorphic to $Z(\Aut(c))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A natural automorphism $\alpha$ of $\mathrm{id}_C$ assigns $\alpha_x\in\Aut(x)$ to each object, and naturality with respect to $\Aut(x)$ forces $\alpha_x$ to be central. Evaluation at $c$ is injective by naturality along a chosen isomorphism $c\to x$, and surjective because a central $z$ defines $\alpha_x:=u_xzu_x^{-1}$, independent of $u_x$ by centrality.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:out} and \ref{thm:centre} compute the automorphism $2$-group of a connected $1$-type: $\pi_0=\Out(G)$ and $\pi_1=Z(G)$.

\begin{corollary}[Abelian case]\label{cor:ab}
If $\pi_1$ is abelian then $\Inn=1$, so $\hAut(K(G,1))\cong\Aut(G)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Rigidity criterion]\label{cor:rigid}
If $G$ is complete (centreless with only inner automorphisms) then $K(G,1)$ is homotopy rigid: $\hAut=1$ and $\Aut(\mathrm{id})=1$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Computations for connected $1$-types}

\begin{theorem}[Degree]\label{thm:circle}
$\hEnd(S^1)\cong(\ZZ,\cdot)$ as monoids: every self-map has a degree $d\in\ZZ$, two self-maps are homotopic iff their degrees agree, degrees multiply under composition, and the identity has degree $1$. A self-map is an equivalence iff its degree is $\pm1$, so $\hAut(S^1)\cong\ZZ/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$S^1$ is a $K(\ZZ,1)$ and $\End(\ZZ)\cong(\ZZ,\cdot)$; $\ZZ$ is abelian so conjugacy is trivial, and Theorem~\ref{thm:monoid} applies. The units of $(\ZZ,\cdot)$ are $\pm1$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:whitehead} identifies units with equivalences.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Torus]\label{thm:torus}
$\hAut(T^n)\cong\GL_n(\ZZ)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$T^n$ is a $K(\ZZ^n,1)$; by Corollary~\ref{cor:ab}, $\hAut\cong\Aut(\ZZ^n)$. Additive automorphisms of an abelian group are its $\ZZ$-linear automorphisms, and those of the free module $\ZZ^n$ are the invertible integer matrices.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Totient theorem]\label{thm:totient}
For every $n\ge1$,
\[ \hAut\bigl(K(\ZZ/n,1)\bigr)\;\cong\;(\ZZ/n)^\times,\qquad \#\hAut\bigl(K(\ZZ/n,1)\bigr)=\varphi(n). \]
In particular $K(\ZZ/1,1)$ and $K(\ZZ/2,1)$ are homotopy rigid, $\#\hAut(K(\ZZ/5,1))=4$, and $\#\hAut(K(\ZZ/p,1))=p-1$ for $p$ prime.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Model $K(\ZZ/n,1)$ as the one-object groupoid on the cyclic group of order $n$. It is abelian, so Corollary~\ref{cor:ab} gives $\hAut\cong\Aut(\ZZ/n)$; an automorphism of $\ZZ/n$ is multiplication by a unit, so $\Aut(\ZZ/n)\cong(\ZZ/n)^\times$, of order $\varphi(n)$.
\end{proof}

This is a bridge between homotopy theory and elementary number theory: the count of homotopy classes of self-homotopy-equivalences of the infinite lens space $K(\ZZ/n,1)$ is a multiplicative arithmetic function of $n$, oscillating in a way no dimension or continuity argument would predict.

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity of $K(S_3,1)$]\label{thm:s3}
Every automorphism of $S_3$ is inner, so $\Out(S_3)=1$ and $K(S_3,1)$ is homotopy rigid; since $Z(S_3)=1$ the identity admits no nontrivial self-homotopy.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$Z(S_3)=1$, so $\Inn(S_3)\cong S_3$ has order $6$; every automorphism permutes the three transpositions and is determined by that permutation, so $\#\Aut(S_3)\le6$. Hence $\Aut=\Inn$. Apply Theorems~\ref{thm:out} and \ref{thm:centre}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nonabelian symmetry group]\label{thm:klein}
For the Klein four group $V=(\ZZ/2)^2$ we have $\hAut(K(V,1))\cong\Aut(V)\cong S_3$, of order $6$ and nonabelian.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$V$ is abelian, so Corollary~\ref{cor:ab} applies; an automorphism of $V$ is exactly an arbitrary permutation of its three involutions.
\end{proof}

\section{Disconnected $1$-types}

Every $1$-type is the disjoint union of its connected components, so the connected results become a complete theory only after the disconnected case is handled. Fix a family $(C_i)_{i\in\iota}$ with $C_i$ connected at $c_i$, and write $\Sigma:=\bigsqcup_{i\in\iota}C_i$.

\begin{theorem}[Totally disconnected $1$-types]\label{thm:discrete}
For a discrete groupoid on a set $\alpha$, $\hEnd\cong\End(\alpha)$, the full transformation monoid, and $\hAut\cong\Sym(\alpha)$; there are no nontrivial self-homotopies of the identity.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A functor between discrete categories is a function on objects, and naturally isomorphic functors agree on objects since the only morphisms are identities. Pass to units for the second claim. All vertex groups, hence all centres, are trivial.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Maps out of a coproduct]\label{lem:coprod}
For any target $D$, restriction along the inclusions is a bijection $\bigl[\bigsqcup_iC_i,D\bigr]\cong\prod_i[C_i,D]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Functors out of a coproduct are families of functors out of the summands, and a natural isomorphism of such is a family of natural isomorphisms; the comparison isomorphisms may be chosen independently on each summand.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Factorization through one component]\label{lem:factor}
Let $A$ be connected at $a$ and $F:A\to\bigsqcup_iC_i$ a functor. Then with $j:=\pr_1F(a)$ there is $P:A\to C_j$ with $F\cong\incl_j\circ P$; and if $\incl_j\circ P\cong\incl_k\circ Q$ then $j=k$ and $P\cong Q$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The image of a connected groupoid is connected and the components of a coproduct are those of the summands, so every $F(x)$ lies over $j$; set $P(x):=\pr_2F(x)$, and likewise on morphisms, using that morphisms of the coproduct between objects over $j$ are morphisms of $C_j$. For uniqueness, the components of a natural isomorphism are morphisms of the coproduct, which forces equal indices, and they then constitute a natural isomorphism $P\cong Q$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:factor} is the technical heart: it turns an arbitrary self-map of a disconnected $1$-type into a matrix.

\begin{definition}[The matrix monoid]\label{def:wr}
Let $\Wr(C)$ be the set of pairs $\langle\sigma,P\rangle$ with $\sigma:\iota\to\iota$ a function and $P_i\in[C_i,C_{\sigma(i)}]$ for each $i$, multiplied by
\[ \langle\sigma,P\rangle\cdot\langle\tau,Q\rangle=\bigl\langle\sigma\circ\tau,\;i\mapsto Q_i\text{ then }P_{\tau(i)}\bigr\rangle, \]
with unit $\langle\mathrm{id},(\clsof{\mathrm{id}_{C_i}})_i\rangle$. One should think of $\langle\sigma,P\rangle$ as a matrix with the single nonzero entry $P_i$ in position $(\sigma(i),i)$ of column $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Matrix normal form]\label{thm:matrix}
For any family $(C_i)$ of connected $1$-types there is a monoid isomorphism
\[ \hEnd\Bigl(\bigsqcup_iC_i\Bigr)\;\cong\;\Wr(C), \]
sending the class of a functor $F$ to the index map $i\mapsto\pr_1F(c_i)$ together with the classes of its components supplied by Lemma~\ref{lem:factor}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Send $\langle\sigma,P\rangle$ to the class of the functor assembled from representatives via the universal property of the coproduct; Lemma~\ref{lem:coprod} gives well-definedness and Lemma~\ref{lem:factor} independence of representatives. Multiplicativity is a direct computation with the coproduct description of composition. Injectivity is the uniqueness half of Lemma~\ref{lem:factor} applied at each $c_i$; surjectivity is its existence half applied to each restriction of $F$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:matrix} classifies \emph{all} self-maps, not only the equivalences, and specialises to Theorem~\ref{thm:discrete} and to Theorem~\ref{thm:monoid}.

Restricting to units gives a bijective index map for each self-equivalence, hence a group homomorphism $\pi:\hAut(\Sigma)\to\Sym(\iota)$ with $\pi(u)(i)=\pr_1F(c_i)$ for any representing functor $F$.

\begin{theorem}[Image: only look-alikes may be permuted]\label{thm:image}
A permutation $\sigma$ lies in $\im\pi$ if and only if $C_i\simeq C_{\sigma(i)}$ for every $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) A representative $F$ is an equivalence by Theorem~\ref{thm:whitehead}, hence induces an isomorphism of vertex groups $\pi_1C_i\cong\pi_1C_{\sigma(i)}$, so $C_i\simeq C_{\sigma(i)}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}. ($\Leftarrow$) Choose equivalences $E_i:C_i\simeq C_{\sigma(i)}$ and assemble along $\sigma$; because $\sigma$ is a bijection the assembled functor is full, faithful and essentially surjective, hence an equivalence with index map $\sigma$.
\end{proof}

Write $\Sym'(\pi_0):=\im\pi=\{\sigma:\forall i,\ C_i\simeq C_{\sigma(i)}\}$, the group of homotopy-type-preserving permutations of the components.

\begin{theorem}[Kernel]\label{thm:kernel}
$\ker\pi\cong\prod_i\Out(\pi_1C_i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Under Theorem~\ref{thm:matrix} a unit lies in $\ker\pi$ iff it is a diagonal matrix; the entrywise inverse of a diagonal unit is diagonal, so each entry is separately invertible. The diagonal embedding $\prod_i\hAut(C_i)\to\hAut(\Sigma)$ is therefore injective with image $\ker\pi$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:out} identifies each factor with $\Out(\pi_1C_i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Structure theorem]\label{thm:structure}
For an arbitrary family of connected $1$-types the sequence
\[ 1\longrightarrow\prod_i\Out(\pi_1C_i)\longrightarrow\hAut\Bigl(\bigsqcup_iC_i\Bigr)\xrightarrow{\ \pi\ }\Sym'(\pi_0)\longrightarrow1 \]
is exact, and the self-homotopies of the identity satisfy $\Aut(\mathrm{id}_\Sigma)\cong\prod_iZ(\pi_1C_i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exactness is Theorems~\ref{thm:image} and \ref{thm:kernel}. For the second claim, restriction along the fully faithful inclusion $C_i\hookrightarrow\Sigma$ sends a self-homotopy of $\mathrm{id}_\Sigma$ to one of $\mathrm{id}_{C_i}$, and assembling a family of such gives a mutually inverse construction; now apply Theorem~\ref{thm:centre} to each summand.
\end{proof}

Since every $1$-type is the disjoint union of its components, Theorem~\ref{thm:structure} determines the automorphism $2$-group of an arbitrary homotopy $1$-type.

\begin{corollary}[Constant family: the wreath product]\label{cor:wreath}
If $C_i=D$ for all $i$ with $D$ connected with fundamental group $G$, then $\pi$ is surjective onto $\Sym(\iota)$, the extension splits, and
\[ \hAut\Bigl(\bigsqcup_{i\in\iota}K(G,1)\Bigr)\cong\Out(G)\wr\Sym(\iota)=\bigl(\iota\to\Out(G)\bigr)\rtimes\Sym(\iota), \]
of order $|\Out(G)|^n\cdot n!$ when $\#\iota=n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Surjectivity is Theorem~\ref{thm:image} with $D\simeq D$; a splitting is given by pure relabelling with identity entries, and the induced action of $\Sym(\iota)$ on $\prod_i\Out(G)$ permutes the factors.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Pairwise inequivalent components]\label{cor:pairwise}
If $C_i\simeq C_j$ implies $i=j$, then $\pi$ is trivial and $\hAut(\bigsqcup_iC_i)\cong\prod_i\Out(\pi_1C_i)$; for finite $\iota$, $\#\hAut=\prod_i\#\Out(\pi_1C_i)$.
\end{corollary}

More generally, if the components of a finite $1$-type fall into homotopy types with multiplicities $m_1,\dots,m_r$ then $\Sym'(\pi_0)\cong\prod_k\Sym(m_k)$ and
\[ \#\hAut\Bigl(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^nC_i\Bigr)=\Bigl(\prod_{i=1}^n\#\Out(\pi_1C_i)\Bigr)\cdot\prod_{k=1}^rm_k! \]
whenever all factors are finite.

\section{Worked examples for disconnected $1$-types}

\begin{theorem}[A rigid two-piece example]\label{thm:two}
The two components of $X=K(\ZZ,1)\sqcup K(\ZZ/3,1)$ are not homotopy equivalent, so no self-homotopy-equivalence interchanges them, and
\[ \#\hAut(X)=\#\Out(\ZZ)\cdot\#\Out(\ZZ/3)=2\cdot2=4. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An equivalence of the components would give $\ZZ\cong\ZZ/3$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}, contradicting finiteness. Corollary~\ref{cor:pairwise} then applies, with $\#\Out(\ZZ)=\#\Aut(\ZZ)=2$ and $\#\Out(\ZZ/3)=\varphi(3)=2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:totient}.
\end{proof}

The four symmetries are the pairs $(\pm1,\pm1)$: reflect the circle or not, invert the lens space or not.

\begin{theorem}[A purely combinatorial example]\label{thm:threeS3}
The disjoint union of three copies of $K(S_3,1)$ has exactly $3!=6$ homotopy classes of self-homotopy-equivalences, all relabellings of the copies.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Out(S_3)=1$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:s3}, so Corollary~\ref{cor:wreath} gives $\hAut\cong\Sym(3)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A mixed example]\label{thm:twoV}
The disjoint union of two copies of $K(V,1)$, $V$ the Klein four group, has $6^2\cdot2=72$ homotopy classes of self-homotopy-equivalences.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\#\Out(V)=6$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:klein}; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:wreath} with $n=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Every permutation is realised in a constant family]\label{thm:realise}
If $D$ is a connected $1$-type and $\iota$ any index set, then every $\sigma\in\Sym(\iota)$ is induced on components by some homotopy self-equivalence of $\bigsqcup_{i\in\iota}D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:image}, since $D\simeq D$.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:two} and \ref{thm:realise} exhibit the two extremes of the exact sequence of Theorem~\ref{thm:structure}.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (Outer automorphism order of a finite group).} Given a finite group $G$ by multiplication table, enumerate operation-preserving bijections $G\to G$ (pruning by generator images and by preservation of element order) to obtain $|\Aut(G)|$; then $|\Inn(G)|=|G|/|Z(G)|$ by the first isomorphism theorem applied to $g\mapsto c_g$, and $|\Out(G)|=|\Aut(G)|/|\Inn(G)|$. With generator-image pruning the cost is $O(|G|^{d+1})$ for a generating set of size $d$.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (Order of the symmetry group of a $1$-type).} Given a finite list of connected components specified by their fundamental groups, partition them into homotopy types by testing group isomorphism (Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}); with multiplicities $m_1,\dots,m_r$ we get $|\Sym'(\pi_0)|=\prod_km_k!$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:structure} the total order is $\bigl(\prod_i|\Out(\pi_1C_i)|\bigr)\prod_km_k!$, while the group of self-homotopies of the identity has order $\prod_i|Z(\pi_1C_i)|$.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (Matrix normal form of a self-map).} For each $i$ compute $\sigma(i)=\pr_1F(c_i)$ and the induced homomorphism $\pi_1C_i\to\pi_1C_{\sigma(i)}$ obtained by transporting $F$ along a chosen path from the basepoint of $C_{\sigma(i)}$ to $F(c_i)$; the pair $\langle\sigma,(\text{class of }\phi_i)\rangle$ is the normal form of Theorem~\ref{thm:matrix}, and $F$ is an equivalence iff $\sigma$ is a bijection and every $\phi_i$ is an isomorphism. This reduces homotopy of self-maps to comparing a permutation and finitely many conjugacy classes of homomorphisms.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What has been achieved}

The results form a closed dictionary: when are two connected $1$-types equivalent (when their $\pi_1$ agree); what are the maps ($\Hom/\text{conj}$); what is the self-map monoid ($\ConjEnd$); which self-maps are equivalences (those with invertible class); what is $\hAut$ ($\Out$); what are the self-homotopies of the identity ($Z$); what are the self-maps of a disjoint union (matrices $\langle\sigma,P\rangle$); what is its $\hAut$ (an extension of $\Sym'(\pi_0)$ by $\prod_i\Out$); and what are its self-homotopies of the identity ($\prod_iZ$). The last three items remove the last hypothesis from earlier descriptions, which required all components to be copies of one $K(G,1)$; the wreath product reappears as the special case where all pieces look alike.

\subsection{Interpretation}

The appearance of $\Out$ rather than $\Aut$ is the precise homotopy-theoretic meaning of ``inner'': an inner automorphism is realised by transporting the basepoint around a loop, which is a homotopy and not new symmetry. The automorphisms lost from $\pi_0$ reappear one level up, since $\Aut(\mathrm{id})\cong Z(G)$ is exactly the kernel of $G\to\Inn(G)$. The automorphism $2$-group $(\Out G,Z(G))$ is thus the crossed module $G\to\Aut(G)$ in disguise, its two invariants being the cokernel and the kernel of that map. Likewise, the structure theorem says all external symmetry is relabelling of look-alike components and all internal symmetry is outer automorphisms, with no interaction beyond the evident permutation action.

\subsection{Sharpness}

No clause of the theory can be dropped: $K(S_3,1)$ shows a nontrivial nonabelian fundamental group can give a completely rigid space; $K(V,1)$ shows abelian $\pi_1$ can give a nonabelian symmetry group; $K(\ZZ/n,1)$ shows the order of $\hAut$ oscillates with $n$ as $\varphi$ does; and $K(\ZZ,1)\sqcup K(\ZZ/3,1)$ shows the permutation part can be trivial even when two components have the same \emph{number} of symmetries, because they have different homotopy types.

\section{Future directions}

This line of work studies when the fundamental group is a complete invariant, in the algebraic model where a homotopy $1$-type is a groupoid and a $K(G,1)$ is a connected groupoid with vertex group $G$. Earlier work classified the objects, the maps, and the automorphism $2$-group of a connected $1$-type, and then $\hAut$ for disconnected $1$-types whose components are copies of a single $K(G,1)$, together with the first nonabelian examples. The present work removes that last hypothesis. Natural continuations:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Non-splitting.} Determine exactly when the extension of Theorem~\ref{thm:structure} splits---it does for a constant family---and describe the classifying cohomology class in $H^2$ of $\Sym'(\pi_0)$ with the induced coefficients.
\item \textbf{Infinite index sets.} For infinite $\iota$ the group $\Sym'(\pi_0)$ carries a natural topology; investigate the resulting topological group structure on $\hAut$.
\item \textbf{More outer automorphism groups.} Extend the library of computed examples to free groups ($\Out(F_n)$), surface groups (mapping class groups, via Dehn--Nielsen--Baer), and finitely generated abelian groups in general.
\item \textbf{Beyond $1$-types.} Move to $2$-types, whose invariants are $\pi_1$, $\pi_2$ as a $\pi_1$-module, and a $k$-invariant in $H^3(\pi_1;\pi_2)$, and describe $\hAut$ as an extension involving the automorphisms of the $k$-invariant.
\item \textbf{Equivariant and fibrewise versions.} Replace groupoids by groupoids over a fixed base, or by $G$-groupoids, and identify the corresponding symmetry groups.
\item \textbf{Effective computation.} Turn the matrix normal form into a decision procedure for homotopy of self-maps of finite $1$-types, and analyse its complexity relative to the group isomorphism problem.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

For homotopy $1$-types, topology and group theory coincide without remainder. The fundamental group classifies connected $1$-types; homotopy classes of maps are conjugacy classes of homomorphisms; the self-map monoid is the conjugacy-class monoid of endomorphisms; the symmetry group of a connected $1$-type is $\Out(\pi_1)$ and its higher symmetry group is $Z(\pi_1)$. Disconnected $1$-types are governed by a matrix monoid, and their symmetry groups are extensions of the type-preserving permutations of the components by the product of the outer automorphism groups. The examples---degree on the circle, $\GL_n(\ZZ)$ on the torus, Euler's totient on lens spaces, rigidity of $K(S_3,1)$---show how the dictionary converts topological questions into arithmetic and combinatorial answers.

\end{document}

